# Analysis of Smartphone Usage Patterns

An Excel and Power BI mini project exploring smartphone users, app usage, screen time, battery consumption and mobile data usage.

## Problem Description and Project Objective

The project converts a tabular smartphone usage dataset into interactive report pages. It compares user groups and device models, summarizes daily usage, and explores relationships between app usage, screen time and battery consumption.
## Tools Used and Dataset Details

- **Microsoft Excel:** dataset preparation, formatting and the App Usage Hours calculation.
- **Microsoft Power BI Desktop:** cards, KPI visuals, charts, slicers and report formatting.
- **Report file:** `Analysis of Smartphone Usage Patterns.pbix`.
- **Source:** [Mobile Device Usage and User Behavior Dataset on Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/valakhorasani/mobile-device-usage-and-user-behavior-dataset).
- **Dataset size:** 700 user records; 11 original columns, plus the added App Usage Hours column.

The source is simulated data. Results describe this dataset and should not be treated as representative findings about all smartphone users.

## Data Cleaning, Preparation and Work Completed

1. Selected the smartphone usage dataset for the mini project.
2. Reviewed the Excel data for missing values and repeated records. Earlier checks of the supplied workbook found no missing values or duplicate user IDs, so all 700 records were retained.
3. Added borders to the Excel data, adjusted alignment and changed the font to Times New Roman.
4. Added **App Usage Hours** to convert daily app usage from minutes into hours.
5. Formatted the new column to two decimal places for readability.
6. Imported the prepared data into Power BI.
7. Used user counts for population comparisons and averages for age, installed apps, usage, screen time, battery drain and data consumption.
8. Created individual visual-building pages and combined the visuals into three numbered dashboard pages.
9. Added page headings, chart titles, labels, colours and slicers.
10. Adjusted card formatting so values such as battery drain display in full rather than as an abbreviation such as 1.53K. Rounded the displayed average number of installed apps to a whole number.

Formatting changes improve presentation; they do not alter the original measurements.

## Formulas and Methods Used

### App Usage Hours

```excel
=D2/60
```

In the supplied Excel layout, column D contains App Usage Time (min/day). The formula was filled down the new column.

**App Usage Hours = App Usage Time (min/day) / 60**

For example, 270 minutes becomes 4.50 hours. Displaying two decimal places does not require rounding the stored calculation.

### Power BI aggregations

The report visuals use Power BI's field aggregation settings. These are not described here as separately authored DAX measures.

| Calculation | Method | Unit |
|---|---|---|
| Total users | Count of user IDs; the source has unique IDs | Users |
| Average age | Average of Age | Years |
| Average apps installed | Average of Number of Apps Installed | Apps |
| Average app usage | Average of App Usage Hours | Hours/day |
| Average screen time | Average of Screen On Time (hours/day) | Hours/day |
| Average battery drain | Average of Battery Drain (mAh/day) | mAh/day |
| Average data usage | Average of Data Usage (MB/day) | MB/day |

The mean is the sum of the values divided by the number of nonblank observations in the current filter context. The installed-app average is approximately **50.68**, displayed as **51** using a zero-decimal format. Its underlying average is unchanged.

